WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.640 --> 00:00:02.920
SPEAKER: Welcome
to "Who's There?"

00:00:02.920 --> 00:00:04.330
a show about Cloud Identity.

00:00:04.330 --> 00:00:10.600
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:10.600 --> 00:00:12.350
In the last episode
of "Who's There?",

00:00:12.350 --> 00:00:14.980
I talked about why Identity
Platform is awesome

00:00:14.980 --> 00:00:17.770
and how to enable it
in your GCP project.

00:00:17.770 --> 00:00:20.950
Today I'm showing you how to
integrate Identity Platform

00:00:20.950 --> 00:00:23.210
into your JavaScript web app.

00:00:23.210 --> 00:00:25.510
If you haven't enabled
Identity Platform yet,

00:00:25.510 --> 00:00:28.480
I recommend you check out the
last episode of "Who's There?"

00:00:28.480 --> 00:00:30.250
before proceeding.

00:00:30.250 --> 00:00:32.950
You also need a web page
that uses JavaScript.

00:00:32.950 --> 00:00:34.960
And if web development
doesn't happen

00:00:34.960 --> 00:00:37.150
to be your jam, then check
out the Getting Started

00:00:37.150 --> 00:00:40.520
videos for iOS and
Android, both linked below.

00:00:40.520 --> 00:00:41.050
All right.

00:00:41.050 --> 00:00:44.810
With the prerequisites out of
the way, let's get started.

00:00:44.810 --> 00:00:46.540
In the Cloud console
for my project,

00:00:46.540 --> 00:00:49.330
I selected the
Identity Platform tab.

00:00:49.330 --> 00:00:52.720
I previously added email
and password authentication.

00:00:52.720 --> 00:00:55.450
If you haven't added this
yet, click Add a Provider

00:00:55.450 --> 00:00:57.890
and select Email Password.

00:00:57.890 --> 00:00:59.770
There are lots of other
providers to explore,

00:00:59.770 --> 00:01:03.010
but today, I'm just
sticking with this one.

00:01:03.010 --> 00:01:04.959
On this provider's
page, you see a link

00:01:04.959 --> 00:01:08.440
in the upper right for
Application Setup Details.

00:01:08.440 --> 00:01:12.400
This pops up a window with brief
getting started information.

00:01:12.400 --> 00:01:15.010
You see "Copy and
paste the snippet below

00:01:15.010 --> 00:01:18.340
at the bottom of your HTML,
before other script tags.

00:01:18.340 --> 00:01:20.250
Learn more."

00:01:20.250 --> 00:01:22.220
Go ahead and click Learn More.

00:01:22.220 --> 00:01:25.010
This takes you to a guide on
getting started with Firebase

00:01:25.010 --> 00:01:27.620
authentication on websites.

00:01:27.620 --> 00:01:29.750
If you've used Firebase
before, you probably

00:01:29.750 --> 00:01:32.030
have some experience
with this documentation.

00:01:32.030 --> 00:01:34.970
But it's entirely possible
you've never even heard

00:01:34.970 --> 00:01:36.930
of Firebase, let alone used it.

00:01:36.930 --> 00:01:39.920
So let's talk about
Firebase for a moment.

00:01:39.920 --> 00:01:42.920
Firebase is a suite of products
to help you build your app

00:01:42.920 --> 00:01:45.020
and grow your user base.

00:01:45.020 --> 00:01:48.500
It includes SDKs for
Objective C and Swift for iOS,

00:01:48.500 --> 00:01:51.750
Java in Kotlin for Android,
JavaScript for web,

00:01:51.750 --> 00:01:55.110
as well as several web
frameworks, like Angular.

00:01:55.110 --> 00:01:58.940
There are game-specific
SDKs, too, for Unity and C++,

00:01:58.940 --> 00:02:01.200
and so much more.

00:02:01.200 --> 00:02:04.250
I could make a whole series
of videos just on Firebase,

00:02:04.250 --> 00:02:07.400
but fortunately, the Firebase
team has already done that.

00:02:07.400 --> 00:02:09.120
If you're interested
in finding out more,

00:02:09.120 --> 00:02:11.840
check out the documentation and
the Firebase YouTube channel,

00:02:11.840 --> 00:02:14.100
both linked below.

00:02:14.100 --> 00:02:17.120
So in many ways, Firebase
is the perfect complement

00:02:17.120 --> 00:02:18.620
to Google Cloud.

00:02:18.620 --> 00:02:21.740
And very broadly speaking,
Google Cloud products focus

00:02:21.740 --> 00:02:25.400
on server-side solutions, like
App Engine, Compute Engine,

00:02:25.400 --> 00:02:29.120
Cloud Functions, Kubernetes
Engine, and Cloud Run.

00:02:29.120 --> 00:02:31.760
And Firebase products focus
on client-side solutions,

00:02:31.760 --> 00:02:35.240
like Client Authentication,
Cloud Messaging, Cloud Storage

00:02:35.240 --> 00:02:37.273
for Firebase, and so on.

00:02:37.273 --> 00:02:38.690
Now, I meant that
it's challenging

00:02:38.690 --> 00:02:41.180
to pin down the relationship
between Google Cloud

00:02:41.180 --> 00:02:42.620
and Firebase.

00:02:42.620 --> 00:02:45.380
My oversimplified explanation
is that Firebase is a way

00:02:45.380 --> 00:02:48.800
to access some Google Cloud
Platform products from a client

00:02:48.800 --> 00:02:50.330
application.

00:02:50.330 --> 00:02:52.190
Specifically for
Identity Platform,

00:02:52.190 --> 00:02:54.140
this explanation works well.

00:02:54.140 --> 00:02:56.270
The Firebase Auth
SDKs enable you

00:02:56.270 --> 00:02:59.150
to access Identity
Platform functionality

00:02:59.150 --> 00:03:01.140
from your client app.

00:03:01.140 --> 00:03:02.930
So with that in
mind, let's head back

00:03:02.930 --> 00:03:05.350
to that Firebase documentation.

00:03:05.350 --> 00:03:08.420
Add the script as
instructed in the configure

00:03:08.420 --> 00:03:13.200
your application instructions
in the Cloud console.

00:03:13.200 --> 00:03:14.840
And that's it.

00:03:14.840 --> 00:03:16.460
Now you can use
the Firebase Auth

00:03:16.460 --> 00:03:20.060
SDK to sign up, log
in, log out, and get

00:03:20.060 --> 00:03:24.410
user info about an
identity platform user.

00:03:24.410 --> 00:03:27.560
Identity Platform gives you
access to many different login

00:03:27.560 --> 00:03:30.620
providers, including
federated identity providers,

00:03:30.620 --> 00:03:33.530
like Google,
Facebook, and Twitter.

00:03:33.530 --> 00:03:36.680
And you can enable several
login methods for a single app,

00:03:36.680 --> 00:03:38.360
allowing your users
to authenticate

00:03:38.360 --> 00:03:40.640
the way that they prefer.

00:03:40.640 --> 00:03:43.220
Some of these login methods
may require additional steps,

00:03:43.220 --> 00:03:46.010
which are listed in the console
when you enable that login

00:03:46.010 --> 00:03:47.270
method.

00:03:47.270 --> 00:03:49.220
See the documentation
below for information

00:03:49.220 --> 00:03:51.680
on how to implement
other login providers.

00:03:51.680 --> 00:03:54.830
For now, let's get back to
implementing email and password

00:03:54.830 --> 00:03:56.810
login.

00:03:56.810 --> 00:03:58.910
In the Cloud console,
create a fake user

00:03:58.910 --> 00:04:01.940
with a test email
and test password.

00:04:01.940 --> 00:04:04.730
Of course, you can also create
a new user from the client,

00:04:04.730 --> 00:04:09.110
but this here is a good way to
get up and running right away.

00:04:09.110 --> 00:04:10.880
See how to create
a user in your web

00:04:10.880 --> 00:04:13.080
app following the link below.

00:04:13.080 --> 00:04:16.279
Sign in your fake user with
the Firebase Auth method

00:04:16.279 --> 00:04:18.870
signInWithEmailAndPassword.

00:04:18.870 --> 00:04:22.460
This function returns a promise
that when resolved is a user

00:04:22.460 --> 00:04:24.050
credential object.

00:04:24.050 --> 00:04:26.000
If there is no error,
you can do whatever

00:04:26.000 --> 00:04:28.100
you need to do with the user.

00:04:28.100 --> 00:04:30.500
Right now, just
log the user's UID

00:04:30.500 --> 00:04:33.150
or display the UID in a div.

00:04:33.150 --> 00:04:36.690
Include a catch block
to handle any errors.

00:04:36.690 --> 00:04:39.880
So it's clear that my example
thus far is not a complete auth

00:04:39.880 --> 00:04:41.000
solution.

00:04:41.000 --> 00:04:43.730
You need a way for users
to create user accounts,

00:04:43.730 --> 00:04:46.802
handle password
resets, and log out.

00:04:46.802 --> 00:04:49.010
And each of these functions
needs some UI development

00:04:49.010 --> 00:04:50.540
as well.

00:04:50.540 --> 00:04:54.020
If you prefer, you can write all
of this functionality yourself

00:04:54.020 --> 00:04:56.810
using the Firebase
guides as a reference.

00:04:56.810 --> 00:04:59.780
But you also have the option
to use the Firebase Auth UI

00:04:59.780 --> 00:05:01.260
module.

00:05:01.260 --> 00:05:05.430
This open source UI was created
by auth specialists at Google.

00:05:05.430 --> 00:05:07.230
It includes all of
the views you would

00:05:07.230 --> 00:05:11.190
need to implement for account
creation, login, logout,

00:05:11.190 --> 00:05:13.710
and password reset
functionality.

00:05:13.710 --> 00:05:17.340
You can incorporate auth UI
in just a few lines of code.

00:05:17.340 --> 00:05:19.800
Personally, whenever
I start a new project,

00:05:19.800 --> 00:05:22.500
I use Firebase UI
first so I can focus

00:05:22.500 --> 00:05:25.260
on the key features of my app
while still protecting data

00:05:25.260 --> 00:05:27.610
through authentication.

00:05:27.610 --> 00:05:30.660
Whether you make your own
UI or use Firebase UI,

00:05:30.660 --> 00:05:32.940
you will very likely need
to know the auth status

00:05:32.940 --> 00:05:35.160
before performing
many app actions.

00:05:35.160 --> 00:05:37.110
For example, you're
going to want

00:05:37.110 --> 00:05:39.570
to check if there is a user
logged in before trying

00:05:39.570 --> 00:05:41.280
to write to a database.

00:05:41.280 --> 00:05:43.900
That's where getting the
current user comes into play.

00:05:43.900 --> 00:05:47.010
Let's look at how to
implement this functionality.

00:05:47.010 --> 00:05:48.600
For each of your
app's pages that

00:05:48.600 --> 00:05:51.150
need information about
the signed-in user,

00:05:51.150 --> 00:05:54.660
attach an observer to the
global authentication object.

00:05:54.660 --> 00:05:57.360
This observer gets called
whenever the user sign-in

00:05:57.360 --> 00:05:59.160
state changes.

00:05:59.160 --> 00:06:03.330
Attach the observer using the
onAuthStateChanged method.

00:06:03.330 --> 00:06:05.280
When a user
successfully signs in,

00:06:05.280 --> 00:06:08.760
you can get information about
the user in the observer.

00:06:08.760 --> 00:06:11.083
Then add the function
that logs out

00:06:11.083 --> 00:06:12.750
so you can see the
state change listener

00:06:12.750 --> 00:06:15.400
closure be triggered again.

00:06:15.400 --> 00:06:17.610
Of course, it's really
unusual to log in

00:06:17.610 --> 00:06:19.620
only to immediately
log out, but this

00:06:19.620 --> 00:06:21.480
is purely for the
sake of example

00:06:21.480 --> 00:06:24.960
so you can see state change
listener get triggered.

00:06:24.960 --> 00:06:27.560
When you run the app,
check the console.

00:06:27.560 --> 00:06:30.900
The console logs show that
the user is logged in,

00:06:30.900 --> 00:06:33.120
and then they are logged out.

00:06:33.120 --> 00:06:34.590
Congratulations.

00:06:34.590 --> 00:06:38.040
You just added identity
platform to a web app.

00:06:38.040 --> 00:06:40.650
Ready to see what else
identity platform can do?

00:06:40.650 --> 00:06:44.070
Check out the Firebase Auth web
guides, auth quick start code,

00:06:44.070 --> 00:06:45.870
and Firebase YouTube channel.

00:06:45.870 --> 00:06:48.780
And be sure to subscribe to the
Google Cloud Platform channel

00:06:48.780 --> 00:06:51.720
so you can be the first to
know about new videos in the

00:06:51.720 --> 00:06:52.650
"Who's There?"

00:06:52.650 --> 00:06:53.800
series.

00:06:53.800 --> 00:06:56.880
Thanks for watching, and we'll
see you on a future episode.

00:06:56.880 --> 00:07:00.530
[MUSIC PLAYING]

